+\subsection*{Auxiliary reader and post-hoc transcript sensitivity}
+A separately preregistered, answer-only Qwen3-VL 8B reader transcribes
+122/128 training-reference images exactly (95.313\%), returns every one of
+16 blank and 16 random-ink controls empty, and produces valid JSON throughout
+calibration. The fixed instruction and anonymous images disclose neither
+questions nor reference answers to the reader. Three responses among 1,015
+unique actor images are invalid, including two that reach the registered
+256-output-token limit. The original diagnostic therefore reports
+\texttt{invalid\_actor\_transcription}; it has no complete fixed-case score.
+
+An explicitly post-hoc sensitivity analysis reuses the first responses
+without retry, repair or new model calls. It retains all 256 cases in each
+condition and assigns each invalid bounded score possible range $[0,1]$.
+The resulting full-case means are below; ranges are identification intervals,
+not confidence intervals. Exact text means normalized transcript agreement
+with any retained reference. Character score is one minus normalized edit
+error, clipped to $[0,1]$.
+\input{transcript_bounds_table.tex}
+K=8's normal-minus-blank character difference is $-0.139$ percentage points,
+with article-bootstrap sensitivity envelope [$-0.962$, 0.638]. The envelope
+uses 2,000 article resamples, seed 20266379, taking the lower endpoint's
+2.5th percentile and upper endpoint's 97.5th percentile. It covers the
+unknown-score assignments within this resampling scheme, not systematic
+reader errors, training-seed variation or reused-development selection.
+It establishes no new model qualification and changes neither failed decision.
+The external reader makes 1,172 calls over 1,092.1 s of owned runtime including
+cleanup; maximum resident GPU memory sampled after requests is 7,278 MiB.
+These costs are separate from actor inference and training. The evaluator
+server and runner exit, its port closes and GPU 0 memory is released.
+
